From e00746edd53655f9d924db3e0cbcb58206f1455f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arne Juul <arnej@vespa.ai>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 12:24:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Allocate Einsum's CUDA intermediates on the stream that uses
 them

Concurrent Run() calls on one CUDA session can corrupt each other's device
memory. Einsum's CUDA device helpers build their intermediates with
Tensor::Create(type, shape, allocator), which calls allocator->Alloc(len) with
no stream. The intermediates are written by queued work (cudaMemcpyAsync,
cudaMemsetAsync on the run's stream) but freed as soon as the unique_ptr
leaves scope in Compute(), while that work is still in flight. Another
request's thread may pick up the same memory on a different stream with
nothing synchronizing the two, surfacing as a sticky cudaErrorIllegalAddress
on the next API call.

Add a Tensor constructor that takes the stream and allocates through
AllocOnStream when the allocator supports it. Bind the run's EinsumCudaAssets
into a lambda on the provider side in ComputeInternal and pass it to the
compute preprocessor and all three typed compute processors, so CreateTensor
can allocate its output on the run's stream without changing the CreateTensor
device helper alias itself - that alias also appears in the
ProviderHostCPU::EinsumTypedComputeProcessor_* virtuals, and the provider
bridge does no version negotiation when loading a provider library, so
widening it could hand an older CUDA provider's three-argument std::function
to a host calling it as a four-argument one. Diagonal, which allocates its
output directly, gets the same treatment; the CPU implementation ignores the
argument. The reduce path allocates inside cuda::ReductionOps::ReduceCompute
instead; Einsum is that function's only caller and it already takes the
stream, so it is fixed here too.

Allocating on that stream is only safe if the stream pointer is one a stream
aware arena may keep. In the plugin build the Stream* behind
OrtStreamAdapter is not always such a pointer: when the host ORT predates
KernelContext_GetSyncStream, GetOrtStream() hands back a stack owned
PluginStreamShim that exists only to carry the native CUDA handle. Passing
it to IAllocator::AllocOnStream would have the plugin arena reinterpret it
as an OrtSyncStream, call SyncStream_GetImpl on an object that is not a
plugin_ep::Stream, and retain the pointer after Compute() returns.

Track the allocation stream separately from the work stream. CudaKernel
gains GetAllocationStream(), which is the compute stream in the framework
build and the host's framework stream - or null, when the host cannot
provide one - in the plugin build; that is the same contract
GetScratchBuffer already relies on through GetFrameworkStreamForStreamArg.
EinsumCudaAssets carries it as alloc_stream_, and CreateTensor, Diagonal and
ReduceCompute tag their output with it while ort_stream_ keeps serving the
CUDA handle, cuDNN and cuBLAS. ReduceCompute takes the allocation stream as a
separate parameter and also forwards it as ReduceComputeCore's
compute_stream, which is only ever used to tag scratch allocations. In the
framework build the two streams are the same pointer, so nothing changes
there.

Tests: a Tensor unit test for the new stream-aware constructor; a CUDA EP
test that wraps the device allocator in a recorder counting Alloc() against
AllocOnStream() and drives all three allocation sites - CreateTensor,
Diagonal, and the ReduceSum path through cuda::ReductionOps::ReduceCompute -
asserting every intermediate is tagged with the run's stream, including the
null allocation stream case.

Other CUDA EP kernels build per-run intermediates the same way (softmax,
expand, identity/sequence copies); those are left for a follow-up.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
